Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:03.669 align:start position:0%
 
good afternoon everybody bonjour  thank you for joining us for this media  availability within term chief bell  joining us are deputy commissioner  chris harkins from the opp and rcmp  deputy commissioner mike zum federal  policing  and i will be the moderator we will be  we will begin with remarks from interim  chief bell and then we will proceed to a  10 to 15 minute question period  we're here to provide you today with an  update on our operations to end the  unlawful protests that we've done so  much damage to our community and so much  harm to our residents the operational  plan is moving ahead as we expected and  the contingencies we put in place are  effectively dealing with the threats  we're seeing on the ground  i thank the ottawa residents for their  patience and support as they watch this  operation unfold  i also thank thank all the planners  officers and emergency personnel many of  whom have come from across the country  to assist us  i'm proud to work with all of them their  assistance has been vital to the success  we are seeing so far in this operation  we're in control of the situation on the  ground and continue to push forward to  clear our streets  this methodical and well-resourced plan  will take time and we are here to see it  through to a safe ending  last night we began to take additional  actions towards implementing our  operation  we moved officers and equipment into key  positions throughout the city and took  up 100 checkpoints around the downtown  core  we began making arrests of key  individuals who were responsible for  organizing these unlawful activities  this morning public order units and a  significant number of officers were  deployed to support our operations  we first focused on nikola on the  nicholas street area where unlawful  protests were gathered with trucks  even this morning all unlawful  lawful protesters were given many many  opportunities to leave  officers then turned to the intersection  of rito and sussex making more arrests  and clearing that location as of 3 pm  today we've arrested 70 people they have  been charged with a mult with multiple  various offenses including mischief  work is underway by the city of ottawa  to remediate and clean up the area that  we've gained back today  we also saw children that were present  in the area we've prepared prepared for  this and have plans to ensure that these  children are unharmed during our  operation  before i take questions i want to  acknowledge that these are not the  images that ottawa residents want to see  ottawa is a welcoming and diverse city  it's a city we're all proud of  we regularly host lawful and peaceful  protests this is family day weekend and  usually our downtown is filled with  people enjoying the city our market and  the canal  we focus we're focused on returning our  city to the place that we all know and  love  we will run this operation 24 hours a  day until the residents and community  have their entire city city back  thank you and i'm happy to take any  name and questions and you have one  question with a follow-up question thank  you joanne tinello cbc news um hi  cheekbell um  you know i was out at mckenzie avenue  and  rito and  many officers of many uh units are out  there holding the line there sort of  pushing  the folks westbound i guess on  wellington um it's getting to be late in  the afternoon and i'm just wondering as  much as you can can you tell us what the  plan is now when i was there anyway for  about an hour everyone was sort of just  standing still uh more or less um  obviously people like to know how long  this would take i don't know if you can  say that but maybe if you can say about  what might happen overnight uh and what  the next steps are thank you  so we we have articulated from the very  beginning we have a very deliberate and  methodical plan that's been extremely  well resourced  i want to thank commissioner karik  commissioner lucky every police chief  across this across canada every police  chief in ontario who have dedicated  resource to help us staff the plan and  see all the officers that you're seeing  today  we from the beginning have indicated  that we would need to move slowly safely  respecting charter rights as we look to  demobilize this demonstration we will  continue to do that and we will work day  and night until this is completed  and just as a follow-up maybe you could  talk a little bit about the kind of  resistance you're seeing is it mostly  peaceful is it obviously the rest of 70  people so  perhaps not but perhaps you could  describe what it's been like for  officers out there and what they're  finding as they meet the protesters  so uh the officers out there  i know everyone's seen many images  through the day have been nothing short  of exceptionally professional all day we  have a plan we articulated that plan to  them and they have executed it extremely  well throughout the day  the level of professionalism that  they're seeing in the face of taunts  slurs towards them is  is challenging but we're seeing their  ongoing professionalism every minute of  every day we'll continue to do that our  officers will continue to work  exceptionally hard to make sure that we  use the least amount of force needed to  actually peacefully end this protest and  move everyone out of our streets  all right chief bill john millen with  the citizen have officers had to help  children's aid remove any children from  families at this point  so we spoke yesterday about the fact we  did have plans  and we're working continually with  children's aid around safety and  security of children in the crowds  we developed those plants hoping that we  weren't going to have to use them  i can tell you to this point we have had  no need to interact with children's aid  as it relates to children within the  crowd what i will tell you is that even  through all the planning it still shocks  and surprises me that we are seeing  children put in harm's way in the middle  of a demonstration where a police  operation is unfolding we will continue  to look after their safety and security  but we implore all the parents who have  kids in there get kids out of there they  do not need to be in the middle of this  it is not a safe place for them chief  what we see downtown right now is do you  expect this to be the posture going  right through the long weekend  as i indicated we will we've started  this operation we talked about uh our  increasing plans yesterday in terms of  securing the downtown core we have a  plan it's well resourced and we will  continue to execute on that plan until  everyone is gone from our streets and  the residents that own those streets get  them back  mario from the canadian press we've seen  um forces from quebec from durham down  there on the front line i'm sorry i'm  sorry i can't hear you thank you mary  wolf from the canadian press  um  there are police officers from quebec  durham various forces down there on the  front line can you give us an idea of  how many police officers are being  mobilized in this operation  so we won't be disclosing any of our  operational numbers but but i'll again  point back to the level of cooperation  and partnership we've had with every  police service every law enforcement  agency across canada unparalleled and  absolutely amazing i'm very proud of how  our community of how our police agencies  and how all other support services have  come together to mobilize to end this  unlawful demonstration  and i noticed that some of the police  from quebec have tear gas are you  prepared to use tear gas and is that a  last resort  so we've developed a well articulated  plan we continue to work with the  protesters the demonstrators to  encourage them to leave the area we have  a very well scripted warning that's  provided to them and up in  every moment that they can leave there  afforded the opportunity to leave we  have well developed plans that will  ensure  ultimate removal of those protesters  from our streets  hi there chris nashgate ctv national  news my first question is did police use  any of the powers confirmed under the  emergencies act to make any arrests  today or did you use the powers of  existing law in the ontario state of  emergency  so i could tell you the  the three levels of government that have  come together to support our efforts in  this um have led us to be able to have  the success we're starting to see right  now uh the emergency measures act uh the  provincial emergency act as well as the  city of ottawa emergency and injunction  that have been have been provided have  created the ability for us to  use powers and use new and  new and existing powers to be able to  properly deal with the demonstration to  ultimately bring it to an end without  the authorities that have been provided  to us through these pieces of  legislation we wouldn't be able to be  doing the work we are today  my other question here is you talked  about the arrests thank you for that um  but  during the live video today on ctv news  channel we saw  in between when there was the hold the  line issue there between the protesters  and the police there was some hitting  amongst one another was there any  injuries  are you reporting any injuries right now  from police and protesters so i can tell  you we have no injuries from the  protester we have one injury to  one of the members of the police team it  was a minor injury that has been treated  here with an extra question but i'm just  wondering if we have an update on or an  estimate as to how many protesters are  actually still here it's mixed in with  media it's kind of hard to tell is there  any update you can give us on that so  again we're not providing numbers a  number of people present number of  operational resources we're using as we  move ahead  protesters yeah no we're not providing  any of those numbers  okay  thank  thank  you  very  much

